    "deletedText": "Mara walked her down the hill to the harbour office, through a town that observed their progress with the stunned attention of a congregation watching the bishop arm in arm with the regicide. At the office door Agnes paused. \"He knew you'd come, you know. Your father. Not the manner of it — he wasn't a prophet, whatever Tomas says after two glasses. But the year before he died he renewed the gauge house clock's contract for thirty years, out of his own pocket, and entered you in the office book as his deputy Keeper, in the appendix where deputies go back to the first lampman. The Board struck the entry out. I kept the page.\" She opened the sailcloth ledger at its ribbon, one final time, and showed Mara the appendix: her own name, in her father's hand, in the column of deputies, with the Board's deletion ruled through it in another hand, and beneath the deletion, in Agnes's small, furious script of twenty-two years ago, the single word: Noted. \"You were never not the Keeper, Miss Vane,\" the harbourmaster said. \"You were only ever out of the room.\"",
